# Units that MUST be enabled on a box at spec. One per line, comments and blanks ignored.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS RATHER THAN A FLAG IN THE INSTALLER.
#
# install-ops.sh installed units and deliberately enabled nothing, and the reason was
# good: enabling zcash-testnet-miner.service means the box starts mining, which is a
# money-path decision an installer must not make on somebody's behalf. So enablement was
# left to a human, and then it silently was not done - 19 of 25 files were not even
# installed, and the reporting timer that would have said so was among them.
#
# The fix is not to let the installer decide. It is to move the decision into the REPO,
# where it goes through review like everything else. The installer then enforces a
# decision somebody made in a pull request, which is a different thing from an installer
# choosing to arm a miner by itself.
#
# Installed-but-not-enabled is the failure this closes: it works until the next reboot
# and then silently does not.
#
# NOT LISTED IS A DECISION TOO. A unit absent from this file is left exactly as the
# operator has it: the installer does not disable anything, because turning something OFF
# on a running box is not a decision a file sync should make either. box-report.sh reports
# units that are enabled without being declared, so that drift is visible rather than
# assumed.

# The external CI gate reads what this publishes, and a box that does not report is
# classified `unknown` rather than healthy. It went stale on 2026-07-31 because the timer
# was never enabled: the report was written once, aged past the 30-minute staleness window,
# and the public probe correctly went red on a faucet that was actually serving fine.
faucet-box-report.timer

# The deploy path itself. Every other line in this file protects something the box does;
# this one protects the box's ability to receive a fix at all. If this timer is not enabled,
# main can move and nothing happens - no error, no alert, just merged work that never lands,
# and the next reboot is when you find out. It was already enabled on the box by hand, which
# is exactly the state this file exists to end: correct today, undeclared, and one reimage
# away from silently not being true.
#
# faucet-autodeploy.service is NOT listed, per the rule at the bottom of this file: it is
# pulled in by this timer and never independently enabled.
faucet-autodeploy.timer

# Container supervision. Without this a crash-looping wallet is nobody's problem until a
# human looks, which is how pre-#175 watchdog code served for a day.
faucet-watchdog.service

# Drift between the repo and the box, reported rather than discovered during an incident.
faucet-drift-report.timer

# Prometheus textfile metrics. Losing these loses the history you diagnose from.
faucet-metrics.timer

# Encrypted backups. The archives are the reason a lost disk is not lost keys.
faucet-backup.timer

# Chain snapshots, so a rebuilt box does not sync from genesis.
zsnap-export.timer

# The Crosslink feature-net node. This file used to list it under DELIBERATELY ABSENT, for
# two reasons, and they have not aged the same way, so both are answered here rather than
# quietly dropped.
#
# The disk reason IS resolved. It read "measured growth extrapolates to roughly the box's
# entire free space", which was true when the state shared a disk with the TAZ chain. The
# state is now on its own 59 GB volume, the unit will not start without that volume
# mounted, and the datadir guard trips at 45 GB. cTAZ filling its storage no longer
# touches the faucet.
#
# The launch reason is NOT resolved, and enabling this does not resolve it. Nothing
# user-facing ships until #328 observes a real drip and the owner says go on the day. That
# gate lives in the faucet's sender toggle, not here: this line starts a node that syncs
# and mines, and a synced node serves nobody by itself. Do not read this as the launch.
#
# What it fixes is the failure this whole file exists for. The node is running on the box
# by the owner's decision, and it is not enabled, so the next reboot stops it and says
# nothing. That is "installed-but-not-enabled works until the next reboot and then quietly
# does not", verbatim, and it would cost days of syncing before anyone noticed.
#
# The honest cost: any box installing this repo now starts syncing a second chain. That is
# the intended behaviour of a box at spec now that we have decided to run cTAZ, and it is
# one line to reverse if that decision changes.
ctaz-node.service

# The Crosslink node's state, written where the faucet container can read it. Enabled
# rather than left to a human because it is the failure this file exists for: without the
# timer running, the status file goes stale and the app reports cannot-verify forever, so
# the cTAZ panel would show nothing on a node that is healthy and at tip. It publishes a
# JSON file and nothing else, so there is no money-path decision in arming it.
ctaz-status.timer

# The channel the faucet container pays through (#409). Enabled rather than left to a
# human for the same reason as ctaz-status.timer: a socket unit is the one type nothing
# else starts, so installed-but-not-enabled means cTAZ answers nothing after the next
# reboot, silently, and the panel says not-ready on a node that is perfectly healthy.
#
# ARMING THIS DOES NOT ARM A MONEY PATH, which is the test every line in this file has to
# pass. It creates a socket that accepts a connection and forwards five allowlisted RPC
# methods. Whether a drip is ever ATTEMPTED is still decided by the faucet's sender
# toggle and by the readiness gate, neither of which this line touches. What it does fix
# is the case where the decision to serve is made and the channel is not there.
ctaz-rpc.socket

# DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and each for its own reason:
#
#   zcash-testnet-miner.service  Mining is always-on by the owner's decision, but it is
#                                still a money path, and it is armed on the box rather
#                                than by a file sync. Listing it here would mean any box
#                                that installs this repo starts mining.
#   faucet-alert@.service        A template. `systemctl enable` on a template without an
#                                instance is an error, not a no-op.
#   *.service behind a *.timer   A service pulled in by its own timer is not independently
#                                enabled, and asking systemd about it reports disabled on a
#                                correct box.
#   ctaz-rpc@.service            Instantiated per connection by ctaz-rpc.socket. Enabling a
#                                template is an error, and the socket above is the thing
#                                that has to survive a reboot.
